### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
Professor Paul Lehner did publicly discuss "pox parties" at a [Science Media Centre press conference on October 23, 2020](https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-8868269/Controversial-pox-parties-used-build-immunity-Covid-19-scientist-says.html), but the Claim cherry-picks his remarks by stripping away essential qualifying context. Lehner explicitly framed pox parties as a "last resort option in the absence of a vaccine" and added the speculative hedge "but we'll have to wait and see," making his actual position far more cautious and conditional than the Claim suggests. Furthermore, the broader scientific consensus at the time strongly opposed deliberate infection strategies: the [WHO called herd immunity through natural infection a "dangerous fallacy"](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-herd-immunity-backed-white-house-dangerous-fallacy-scientists-n1243415), 80 scientists signed an open letter in *The Lancet* opposing it, and [multiple COVID-19 vaccines were already in late-stage Phase III trials](https://cepi.net/news_cepi/321-vaccine-candidates-against-covid-19-now-in-development/) with imminent authorization—making the deliberate infection of children an unnecessary and risky proposition that Lehner himself did not outright endorse.